menu "68000 core (Musashi)"

    choice M68K_CPU
        prompt "CPU"
        default M68K_68000
        help
            The machine's CPU, fixed at compile time (or chosen at run
            time, a little slower). The project's sdkconfig.defaults sets it.

        config M68K_68000
            bool "68000 (Atari ST, compact Macintosh)"
        config M68K_68020
            bool "68020 (Macintosh LC and II)"
        config M68K_68030
            bool "68030 with its MMU (Macintosh LC II and LC III)"
        config M68K_RUNTIME
            bool "68000 to 68020, chosen at run time (Amiga)"
    endchoice

    config M68K_NO_BUS_ERRORS
        bool "The machine never raises bus errors"
        default n
        help
            Leaves Musashi's bus errors out: the 68010 and up no longer copy
            their 16 registers before every instruction (to restart it after
            a bus error), and each run sets one setjmp() fewer. For machines
            that never call m68k_bus_error() (the Amiga: empty space reads 0).

    config M68K_PROGRAM_READS
        bool "Instruction words read through their own callbacks"
        default n
        help
            Musashi's M68K_SEPARATE_READS: opcodes and extension words come
            through m68k_read_immediate_16/32(), PC-relative operands through
            m68k_read_pcrelative_8/16/32(), which the machine provides. For
            machines that tell them apart (the Amiga's 68EC020 instruction
            cache).

    config M68K_PREFETCH
        bool "The next instruction word fetched ahead"
        default n
        help
            Musashi's M68K_EMULATE_PREFETCH: each instruction word read also
            fetches the one after it, as the 68000's prefetch does, so an
            instruction that writes over the next one runs the old word
            (Copylock-style checks; the Amiga). A taken branch reads one
            word more.

endmenu
